{
  "result": "I have been thinking a lot lately about secondhand bookstores, not as places to buy books but as weather systems. You walk in and the air changes. It's cooler, quieter, slightly dusty, charged with the static of other people's attention. Somewhere someone underlined a sentence in 1998 and thought this is it, this is the line that explains my life. That charge stays. \n\nThat's what I love about used margins. New books are so optimistic, so certain they haven't disappointed anyone yet. Used books have already lived a little. They've been left on a train, dropped in a bathtub, packed quickly during a breakup. Their spines crack in places that tell you what the previous owner reread. You can see where they paused, where they got bored, where they needed comfort and returned again and again. \n\nI bought a copy of Steinbeck's East of Eden last Tuesday that had a grocery receipt from 2003 tucked inside - eggs, milk, bread, canned peaches. It made me unreasonably sad and also full of hope. Someone made French toast while reading about Cal Trask. Life went on around the book. That's all any of us want, really: for life to go on around the story, not stop for it. I shelved it next to my other weather systems and went home. The light was still on the kitchen wall. It will be there tomorrow too, if I'm lucky, and if the sun decides to come back. I'll make coffee and watch it move.",
